--!strict
--[[
	MapData — the world map's single configuration file. Presentation only: nothing here affects
	the simulation. Province ownership, development and adjacency stay in ProvinceDefinitions and
	the bookmarks; this file only says how the map is drawn and which province a click selects.

	Coordinates are map units in a 1000 x 1900 viewBox (x right, y down). The renderer keeps the
	aspect ratio at every zoom level.

	Contents
	  houses          the great houses shown on the map: palette, sigil, words, label and crest spots
	  regions         one per great house: the owning house and its outline (one or more paths)
	  subTerritories  every region is split into 4-10 unnamed sub-territories, each with its own
	                  outline, a shade (an index into its house's shadeColors) and the game province
	                  it selects when clicked. An optional houseId overrides the region's house.
	  locations       castles, cities, ports and landmarks with an icon and a small label
	  specialFeatures Beyond the Wall, the Wall, the Gift and the Stepstones: drawn, never owned

	A path is a flat list of coordinates { x1, y1, x2, y2, ... } describing a simple polygon.

	The outlines between "BEGIN GENERATED GEOMETRY" and "END GENERATED GEOMETRY" are traced from
	the reference map by tools/mapgen/generate_map.py, which rewrites only that block.
	Neighbouring outlines share their border points exactly, so there are no gaps or overlaps.

	How to add a new great house
	  1. Add an entry to `houses` with a unique id, its palette (primaryColor plus 4-6
	     shadeColors), a sigil description, its words and the label and crest positions.
	  2. Add its sigil art to SigilArt.luau under the same id (until then the crest shows the
	     plain shield in the house's primary colour).
	  3. Give it land: either a new entry in `regions` plus sub-territories with that regionId, or
	     move existing ones by changing a region's houseId.
	  4. Run the tests (tests/specs/k_map.spec.luau checks ids, colours and references).

	How to add a minor house inside a great house's region (for example House Bolton in the North)
	  1. Add the house to `houses` as above (its label and crest positions can be omitted with
	     `minor = true`, which keeps the map uncluttered).
	  2. Set `houseId = "bolton"` on the sub-territories it holds. Those sub-territories are then
	     shaded from its palette and named after it in tooltips; the region keeps its great house.
]]

export type Point = { x: number, y: number }
export type Path = { number }

export type House = {
	id: string,
	name: string,
	seat: string,
	regionName: string,
	primaryColor: string,
	shadeColors: { string },
	sigil: string,
	words: string,
	minor: boolean?,
	label: Point?,
	crest: Point?,
}

export type Region = {
	id: string,
	name: string,
	houseId: string,
	paths: { Path },
}

export type SubTerritory = {
	id: string,
	regionId: string,
	provinceId: string,
	shade: number,
	houseId: string?,
	paths: { Path },
}

export type LocationKind = "seat" | "city" | "port" | "landmark"

export type Location = {
	id: string,
	name: string,
	x: number,
	y: number,
	type: LocationKind,
	provinceId: string?,
}

export type SpecialFeature = {
	id: string,
	name: string,
	color: string,
	label: Point?,
	paths: { Path },
}

type Geometry = {
	subTerritories: { SubTerritory },
	regions: { [string]: { Path } },
	features: { [string]: { Path } },
}

